---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Card Keeper Privacy Policy"
  description="How the Card Keeper app for iPhone and Mac stores payment cards and barcodes in your keychain, and what it never sends anywhere."
  lang="en"
>
  <article class="privacy-policy">
    <nav class="locale-switch" aria-label="Language selector">
      <a href="/cards/privacy/" class="locale-pill is-active" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a>
      <a href="/cards/privacy/ko/" class="locale-pill" hreflang="ko" lang="ko">한국어</a>
      <a href="/cards/privacy/ja/" class="locale-pill" hreflang="ja" lang="ja">日本語</a>
      <a href="/cards/privacy/zh-hans/" class="locale-pill" hreflang="zh-Hans" lang="zh-Hans">简体中文</a>
      <a href="/cards/privacy/zh-hant/" class="locale-pill" hreflang="zh-Hant" lang="zh-Hant">繁體中文</a>
    </nav>

    <h1>Card Keeper Privacy Policy</h1>

    <section>
      <h2>Summary</h2>
      <p>Card Keeper keeps your payment cards and barcode cards in the keychain on your iPhone and Mac. The app has no account, no server, no analytics and no advertising. We, the developer, cannot see or access anything you save in Card Keeper.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What Card Keeper Stores</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Payment cards:</strong> card number, expiry date, cardholder name, CVC (optional) and a nickname you choose.</li>
        <li><strong>Barcode cards</strong> (for example membership or library cards): the barcode value, its format (such as QR or EAN-13) and a nickname.</li>
        <li>So that your list can appear without asking you to authenticate, the nickname, card brand, last four digits and expiry date of each payment card are kept separately from the card number, cardholder name and CVC.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where It Is Stored</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Everything is saved in the keychain on your device.</li>
        <li>If iCloud Keychain is turned on for your Apple Account, your cards and barcodes sync to your other devices through your own iCloud Keychain. Apple encrypts iCloud Keychain end to end, and we have no access to it.</li>
        <li>You can keep any card or barcode out of iCloud Keychain by turning on the option to keep it on this iPhone or this Mac only, either when you save it or later on its detail screen. It is then stored only on that device.</li>
        <li>To tell your other devices that something changed, Card Keeper writes a random change token to iCloud key-value storage in your iCloud account. The token contains no card or barcode data.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Authentication</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Before Card Keeper shows, copies or fills a card number, cardholder name or CVC, it asks you to authenticate: Face ID, Touch ID or your passcode on iPhone, and Touch ID or your login password on Mac.</li>
        <li>The nickname, card brand, last four digits and expiry date are shown without authentication. Barcode cards are also shown without authentication.</li>
        <li>Face ID and Touch ID are handled by the system. Card Keeper receives only whether authentication succeeded, never your biometric data.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Camera and Photos</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>When you read a card or barcode with the camera, or choose an image, Card Keeper recognises the text or barcode on your device.</li>
        <li>Camera frames and images are not saved and are not sent anywhere. Only the values you confirm are saved.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Safari Extension</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Card Keeper's extension script is present on the pages of the websites you allow it to access, which is why Safari warns that the extension can read webpages. It reads a page's form fields only when you open Card Keeper on that page.</li>
        <li>When you open Card Keeper from Safari on a checkout page, the extension looks at the form fields on that page to find card fields, such as their labels and autofill hints, and reads the page address.</li>
        <li>It fills only on https pages, and only after you choose a card and authenticate on your device.</li>
        <li>Card details pass from the keychain to the page through the extension on your device. Nothing from the page or from your cards is sent to us or to anyone else, and the extension does not keep any page data.</li>
        <li>You can turn the extension off, or change which websites it may access, in Safari settings at any time.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Barcode Widget (iPhone)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The widget shows the barcode you choose on your Home Screen. It reads that barcode from the keychain on your iPhone.</li>
        <li>While your iPhone is locked, the widget hides the barcode image and value. The barcode's nickname and format stay visible.</li>
        <li>To remember which barcode it last showed, the widget keeps only that barcode's identifier on your iPhone.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Clipboard</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Values you copy from Card Keeper stay on the device where you copied them and are not shared with your other devices through Universal Clipboard.</li>
        <li>A copied value is removed from the clipboard after 60 seconds. On Mac, Card Keeper also removes it when you quit the app. If you have copied something else in the meantime, Card Keeper leaves that alone.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What We Do Not Do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not run servers, and the app makes no network requests of its own.</li>
        <li>The app contains no analytics, advertising or tracking code. The card number recognition model in the app comes from the open-source stripe-ios project; it runs on your device and sends nothing.</li>
        <li>We do not collect, sell or share your personal information. We cannot access the cards and barcodes you save.</li>
        <li>This policy covers the Card Keeper app. This website uses its own analytics and advertising, described in the <a href="/privacy/">site privacy policy</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Deleting Your Data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Delete a card or barcode on its detail screen in Card Keeper. If it syncs through iCloud Keychain, it is deleted from all your devices that use iCloud Keychain.</li>
        <li>Removing the app does not delete items from the keychain, and synced items stay in your iCloud Keychain. If you reinstall Card Keeper, your saved items appear again. To remove your data, delete each card and barcode in Card Keeper before you remove the app.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>Card Keeper is not directed at children. Because Card Keeper collects no personal information from anyone, it collects none from children.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Changes to This Policy</h2>
      <p>If this policy changes, we will update this page and the effective date below.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>For privacy questions, please contact <a href="mailto:oozoofrog@gmail.com">oozoofrog@gmail.com</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Effective Date</h2>
      <p>November 18, 2026</p>
    </section>

    <nav class="page-nav">
      <a href="/cards/" class="nav-link">Home</a>
      <a href="/cards/support/" class="nav-link">Support</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .privacy-policy {
    max-width: 860px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 32px 24px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);
    border-radius: 22px;
    box-shadow: 0 12px 36px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08);
    backdrop-filter: blur(22px) saturate(150%);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(22px) saturate(150%);
  }

  .locale-switch {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }

  .locale-pill {
    display: inline-flex;
    min-height: 36px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 0 14px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
    color: #714855;
    border: 1px solid rgba(158, 49, 71, 0.08);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .locale-pill.is-active {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #9e3147, #bf4960);
    color: #fff;
  }

  .locale-pill:hover {
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
    color: #9e3147;
  }

  .locale-pill.is-active:hover {
    color: #fff;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.8rem);
    color: #3f1c27;
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 14px;
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--frog-green);
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    color: #3f1c27;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  p,
  li {
    color: #5d4650;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  li + li {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  .page-nav {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    padding-top: 18px;
    border-top: 1px solid #eadfe2;
  }

  .nav-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 42px;
    padding: 0 18px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(112, 168, 80, 0.92), rgba(74, 142, 58, 0.92));
    color: #fff;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .nav-link:hover {
    color: #fff;
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
    transform: translateY(-1px);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .privacy-policy {
      padding: 24px 18px;
    }

    .locale-switch {
      justify-content: flex-start;
    }

    .page-nav {
      flex-direction: column;
    }
  }
</style>
